1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:06,910
Have you ever, like, tried to prove to leadership that a massive government tech overhaul is actually working?

2
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:14,000
I mean, you wanna show progress, but it's so easy to just drown them in a fifty-page report of completely meaningless numbers.

3
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:23,640
Oh, it's the classic trap. You, uh, you desperately need to show the people holding the purse strings that their money is well spent, but you just end up burying the actual reality.

4
00:00:23,660 --> 00:00:26,630
Right, in a mountain of data and charts that basically don't mean a thing.

5
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:28,760
So let's cut right through that noise.

6
00:00:30,220 --> 00:00:35,580
Welcome to today's deep dive. I'm your host, and joining me today to unpack this is our resident expert.

7
00:00:35,740 --> 00:00:39,520
Great to be here, and yeah, this is a topic I am very passionate about.

8
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:45,860
So today, we're diving into the factual metrics that measure the true impact of a national enterprise architecture.

9
00:00:46,230 --> 00:00:53,640
And just to ground us, when we say enterprise architecture, or EA, we're basically talking about the master blueprint of an organization's IT systems.

10
00:00:53,780 --> 00:00:55,600
Yeah, exactly. It's how everything connects.

11
00:00:55,660 --> 00:01:05,420
Right. So our mission today is to distill the success of that blueprint down to a handful of honest numbers and, you know, spot the busy work metrics that teams use to

12
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:06,800
hide stalled progress.

13
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:14,200
Because before you can prove the EA's value to a budget authority, you really have to know which numbers actually reflect reality.

14
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:17,510
I mean, there are four essential metrics that carry the real signal here:

15
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:21,550
coverage, reuse rate, open exceptions, and decisions.

16
00:01:21,780 --> 00:01:25,440
Okay, let's take those apart a bit 'cause I can immediately see a temptation here.

17
00:01:25,450 --> 00:01:30,850
Oh. If I'm reviewing this, couldn't I just, uh, count how many systems my team has mapped out to show progress?

18
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:35,240
Like point to a massive stack of diagrams and say, "Look at all this coverage."

19
00:01:35,700 --> 00:01:37,840
You could, but that is exactly the trap.

20
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,600
Coverage isn't about how many pages you've generated.

21
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:44,880
It's about how much of your government's tech is actually documented in the repository.

22
00:01:44,910 --> 00:01:45,880
And it has to be current, right?

23
00:01:46,040 --> 00:01:47,420
Crucially current, yes.

24
00:01:48,040 --> 00:01:53,460
Because if your map of the IT landscape is outdated, well, it's essentially useless.

25
00:01:53,700 --> 00:02:01,480
That makes total sense. And looking at the other metrics, it seems like they aren't just measuring progress, they're almost acting like early warning systems.

26
00:02:01,790 --> 00:02:03,020
Take decisions, for example.

27
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:06,140
Yeah, that tracks how fast the board makes gate decisions.

28
00:02:06,420 --> 00:02:10,299
Basically, the checkpoints where new tech projects get approved or blocked.

29
00:02:10,340 --> 00:02:17,040
Because I'd imagine if that approval process takes too long, frustrated departments will just bypass the architecture entirely, right?

30
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:19,280
They'll just build their own shadow IT to get things done.

31
00:02:19,740 --> 00:02:25,720
Spot on. If the board is too slow, the EA becomes a bottleneck, and projects just route right around it.

32
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:28,920
And, uh, the same logic applies to open exceptions.

33
00:02:28,960 --> 00:02:29,640
Which are the waivers.

34
00:02:30,060 --> 00:02:37,020
Right. It tracks the number and age of outstanding waivers, official permissions for a project to break the architecture rules.

35
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:45,200
If you have a growing pile of old unresolved waivers, it means system fragmentation is quietly sneaking right back into your government.

36
00:02:45,280 --> 00:02:46,780
Okay, so we have our core four.

37
00:02:47,720 --> 00:02:54,170
But if we connect this to the bottom line, say you only have thirty seconds in an elevator with a minister or budget authority,

38
00:02:54,880 --> 00:02:58,040
what is the single metric you put in front of them to secure your funding?

39
00:02:58,180 --> 00:03:06,620
Oh, it has to be the reuse rate. You look at the projects that came through those gate decisions and ask how many consumed a shared building block instead of building their own.

40
00:03:06,680 --> 00:03:12,300
Wait, so like using an existing identity platform instead of coding a brand new one from scratch?

41
00:03:12,340 --> 00:03:17,540
Exactly. Every time a department does that, the country pays for it once instead of, you know, twenty times.

42
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:26,500
Oh, I love that. So it's kind of like an office buying one commercial espresso machine for the break room instead of reimbursing fifty different employees for their own individual coffee makers.

43
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:36,600
That is a perfect analogy. When you track that over a year, a rising reuse rate is really the closest thing you have to hard proof that the EA is paying for

44
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:42,540
itself. It takes this highly abstract argument that good planning enables reuse.

45
00:03:42,580 --> 00:03:45,170
And turns it into a tangible number that actually goes up.

46
00:03:45,440 --> 00:03:48,060
Yes. That's the metric that protects your funding.

47
00:03:48,340 --> 00:03:52,340
But knowing what to measure is really only half the battle, isn't it?

48
00:03:52,380 --> 00:03:56,440
The other half is presenting the truth without falling back on what we'd call vanity metrics.

49
00:03:56,600 --> 00:03:59,280
Oh, you've got to avoid vanity metrics at all costs.

50
00:03:59,540 --> 00:04:03,370
The number of diagrams drawn, pages written, meetings held.

51
00:04:03,420 --> 00:04:05,540
I mean, these measure activity, not effect.

52
00:04:05,820 --> 00:04:10,510
Because a stalled EA program can easily hide behind a thousand pages of documentation.

53
00:04:10,560 --> 00:04:17,760
Exactly. The golden rule is this: if a metric would still go up while the architecture quietly stopped mattering, well, it's the wrong metric.

54
00:04:17,920 --> 00:04:22,320
I get that. But there is a massive psychological temptation here.

55
00:04:22,360 --> 00:04:30,440
If you're going in front of a minister, human nature says you want to present a perfectly green, flawless quarterly scorecard to prove you're doing a great job.

56
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:40,460
You really have to resist that urge. You should be reporting those four core metrics on a single page every quarter to the board and the minister, and you absolutely have to include

57
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:41,420
the bad news.

58
00:04:41,500 --> 00:04:44,520
Like a coverage gap in a sector that won't cooperate.

59
00:04:44,640 --> 00:04:49,780
Yes, or the aging exceptions that some powerful ministry just refuses to close.

60
00:04:50,500 --> 00:04:56,250
A scorecard that is honestly amber, you know, showing some warning signs, is what builds long-term trust.

61
00:04:56,300 --> 00:04:57,800
Because it proves you're telling the truth.

62
00:04:57,960 --> 00:05:02,219
Right, which keeps the EA funded across different political administrations.

63
00:05:02,380 --> 00:05:05,900
A scorecard that is always perfectly green is one nobody believes the second time.

64
00:05:06,180 --> 00:05:07,420
So to wrap this up,

65
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:14,280
skip the busy work metrics. If you want to prove your tech overhaul is actually working, track coverage that is current,

66
00:05:14,900 --> 00:05:17,800
reuse rates, open exceptions, and gate decisions.

67
00:05:18,100 --> 00:05:19,950
You want to measure effect, not just activity.

